The '''Scientific Schism''', also known as the '''New Oriental Schism''', or the '''Schism of 2101''' (Latin: ''Magnum schisma scientificum''), was a split within the Catholic Church lasting from 2101 to 2133 in which bishops residing in Rome and Licheń both claimed to be the true pope. The schism was driven by blowback from [[Sixth Great Awakening|Third Vatican Council]] the and the earlier [[Proclamation of Sapere aude]]. There was also a political element to the schism with the Licheń papacy being closely associated with the Polish government and the See of Rome being much closer to Mexico. These rival claims to the papal throne damaged the prestige of the office.

The schism was finally resolved when the Licheń claimant Pius XIII called the Council of Seville (2130–2134) after the death of Pope Adrian VII. The Council arranged the renunciation of the Licheń antipope Pius XIII, renounced many of the positions of the Third Council while maintaining the Biblical translations compiled by that council, and elected John-Paul III as the new pope reigning from Rome.

